  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 535 AM EST MON NOV 2 2026\n\nFrom Tuesday night through Wednesday, the upper level ridge axis \nshifts to the Gulf to the Southeast U.S. Coast. Meanwhile the\nupper trough axis remains west of eastern KY through Wednesday as\nthe upper level low within it evolves over portions of the MS\nValley. A lead shortwave trough rotating around it should reach\nthe lower OH Valley tonight and gradually move across the \nCommonwealth through Wednesday evening/night. At the same time the\nupper level low tracks toward the Great Lakes. The associated sfc\nfrontal zone should progress across the Commonwealth. Deep\nsoutherly flow with this system will transport deep moisture into\neastern KY, with mean PW per the 00Z LREF remaining generally in \nthe 90th percentile or higher on Wednesday with the PW peaking \nbetween the 95th and 99th percentile range during the afternoon \nand evening. This system will have better forcing than the weekend\nsystem as well and showers will be widespread and rainfall totals\ngreater. The current forecast storm total ranges from around 0.75\nin the rain-shadowed areas downstream of the Pine/Black Mtn \nvicinity to the 1 to 1.5 inch range for locations further west. \n00Z LREF mean rainfall totals were in the 0.50 to 0.8 inch range \nthough higher end amounts for the 24 hour period ending at 7 AM \nEST on Thursday though higher end amounts (95th percentile) were \nin the 1 to 1.75 inch range per the 00Z LREF. WPC has maintained \na marginal risk of excessive rain during that period, though our \nrecent dry trend is a mitigating factor. \n\nIn addition to the showers and potential for some thunder, winds\naloft will increase as the system approaches and gusts in the\nhigher terrain near the VA border are likely to exceed NBM\ndeterministic values. Wind gusts in the highest terrain were\nadjusted upward for Tuesday night to early Wednesday. \n\nChances for showers will decrease behind the midweek system, but \nlinger perhaps as late as Thursday night or early Friday. There \nremains some uncertainty with the overall pattern to end the week,\nbut a generally drier period is currently anticipated from later \nFriday through Saturday night with temperatures trending colder \nunder broad troughing over the central to eastern Conus. Some \nfrost or near freezing temperatures could also occur Friday night \nand/or Saturday night. Another upper low may approach the mid MS \nValley/Lower OH Valley vicinity near or after the end of the \nperiod and result in a return of unsettled weather.",
